19:["$","section",null,{"data-reveal":true,"className":"border-b-hairline-soft py-20","children":["$","$L29",null,{"kind":"cases","items":[{"frontmatter":{"title":"How a national flower and gift retailer turned scattered delivery photos into instant proof","slug":"delivery-evidence-automation-google-workspace","category":"logistics","services":["custom-software-development","digital-transformation"],"techStack":[{"name":"Google Apps Script","category":"Automation"},{"name":"Google Drive","category":"Storage"},{"name":"Google Sheets","category":"Operations"},{"name":"Google Workspace","category":"Platform"}],"year":2025,"country":"Mexico","language":"en","translationKey":"delivery-evidence-automation","featured":true,"status":"published","publishedDate":"2026-07-18","lastUpdated":"2026-07-18","client":{"name":"National flower and gift retailer with same-day delivery","sector":"Retail · Flowers, gifts and same-day delivery","geography":"México · nationwide","bio":"A leading Mexican retailer of flowers, gifts and occasion products, delivering nationwide on the same day. Hundreds of daily deliveries are fulfilled by a distributed network of external couriers — motorcyclists and drivers — which makes proof of delivery a core part of the customer promise, not a back-office detail.","anonymous":true,"sameAs":[]},"hero":{"lead":"Hundreds of same-day deliveries a day, every one requiring photographic proof — and that proof lived in WhatsApp threads, couriers' personal phones and unnamed folders. When a customer complained, Customer Service went hunting. We automated capture, filing and retrieval inside the Google Workspace the company already owned, without asking a single courier to learn a new app.","visualType":"image","visualAlt":"Case study cover for a client under privacy","stats":[{"value":"Same-day","label":"Nationwide delivery model","context":"hundreds of deliveries daily"},{"value":"0","label":"New apps for couriers","context":"they only upload the photo"},{"value":"1 link","label":"Per sales order","context":"auto-linked in the master sheet"},{"value":"Zero","label":"Manual filing steps","context":"fully unattended pipeline"}],"visual":"/case-studies/anonymous-cover.webp"},"diagnosis":{"challenges":["Delivery evidence scattered across WhatsApp threads, personal phones and unstructured folders","No reliable way to tie a photo back to the sales order it belonged to","Customer Service spent significant time hunting for images during a complaint","Slow claim resolution directly hurt internal service-quality metrics","Manual filing introduced human error — misplaced, misnamed or lost evidence","External couriers rotate; any solution requiring training would decay quickly"],"businessImpact":"Every claim opened without evidence at hand turned a two-minute answer into a search. That delay compounded across hundreds of daily deliveries, degrading response times and the internal quality metrics the operation is measured on.","previousAttempts":"The evidence requirement was already policy — the gap was never intent, it was that the process depended on people manually organizing files at the end of a delivery shift."},"phases":[{"name":"Operational diagnosis","description":"Before writing code we followed the evidence end to end: how a courier captures the photo, where it lands, who touches it next, and what Customer Service actually needs at the moment a claim arrives. The decisive finding was that the bottleneck was not capture — couriers were taking the photos. It was everything after: naming, filing and retrieval.","deliverables":["End-to-end map of the delivery-evidence flow","Inventory of where evidence was actually landing","Definition of the retrieval moment that mattered (the claim)"]},{"name":"Solution design inside the existing stack","description":"The company already ran on Google Workspace and administered every sales order in a master Google Sheet. Rather than introduce a new platform, we designed the automation to live where the work already happened — so adoption cost was effectively zero and no license or vendor was added.","deliverables":["Architecture designed on Google Apps Script + Drive + Sheets","Folder and naming convention keyed to the order number","Zero-training interaction model for couriers"]},{"name":"Build and automation","description":"We built the pipeline in Google Apps Script: the courier uploads a photo, and the system identifies the order number, creates a dedicated folder for that order, files the images, generates the folder link and writes that link into the correct row of the master sheet. No human step in between.","deliverables":["Apps Script automation for identification, filing and linking","Automatic per-order folder creation in Drive","Link write-back into the master sales-order sheet"]},{"name":"Rollout and handover","description":"Because couriers' behaviour didn't change — upload the photo, nothing else — rollout was about validating the pipeline against real daily volume and making sure Customer Service knew that the answer now lived one click away in the row they already had open.","deliverables":["Validation against live delivery volume","Customer Service walkthrough of the new lookup path"]}],"kpis":[{"label":"Manual evidence search","value":"Eliminated","context":"no more hunting through WhatsApp or personal phones"},{"label":"Claim resolution time","value":"Reduced","context":"significantly — evidence is retrieved from the order row, not searched for"},{"label":"Delivery traceability","value":"Improved","context":"notably — every order has its own evidence folder"},{"label":"New apps for couriers","value":"Zero","context":"the interaction stayed a photo upload"}],"takeaways":[{"title":"The bottleneck is rarely where the work is visible","body":"Couriers were already doing their part — the photos existed. The cost lived in the invisible steps afterward: naming, filing and finding. Automating the visible step would have changed nothing.","applicability":"Any operation where field staff already generate the data but the office pays to organize it."},{"title":"The best platform is often the one already paid for","body":"Google Workspace was already licensed, already administering every sales order, and already familiar to the team. Building inside it removed the adoption curve, the procurement conversation and the integration risk in one decision.","applicability":"Mid-size operations with an established Workspace or Microsoft 365 footprint."},{"title":"Design for turnover, not for the ideal user","body":"External couriers rotate. A solution requiring training would have degraded within months. Keeping the courier's interaction to a single photo upload made the automation durable by design.","applicability":"Any process depending on contractors, seasonal staff or high-turnover roles."},{"title":"Put the answer where the question is asked","body":"Customer Service already worked from the master sheet. Writing the evidence link into that exact row meant no context switch, no second system and no training — the answer simply appeared where they were already looking.","applicability":"Support and back-office teams that live inside one operational system of record."}],"faqs":[{"question":"How do you automate proof of delivery without a dedicated logistics app?","answer":"By automating the steps after capture rather than the capture itself. In this project couriers kept uploading a photo exactly as before; Google Apps Script then identified the order number, created a dedicated Drive folder, filed the images, generated the link and wrote it into the master Google Sheet. No dedicated app, no courier training, no new vendor."},{"question":"Why use Google Apps Script instead of building a custom platform?","answer":"Because the company already ran its operation on Google Workspace and administered every sales order in a master Sheet. Apps Script let the automation live inside that existing environment, which removed adoption cost, avoided a new license and kept the operation on tools the team already trusted. A new platform would have solved the same problem while adding training, procurement and integration risk."},{"question":"What is the real cost of disorganized delivery evidence?","answer":"It shows up as time. Every claim without evidence at hand converts a short answer into a search across WhatsApp threads, personal phones and folders. Multiplied across hundreds of daily deliveries, that search time degrades response speed and the internal service-quality metrics the operation is measured on — before counting the claims that can't be resolved at all because the evidence was lost."},{"question":"Does this approach work for other last-mile operations?","answer":"Yes. The pattern — capture stays unchanged, filing and retrieval become automatic, and the result lands in the system of record the team already uses — applies to any operation where field staff generate evidence that the office later needs to find: last-mile delivery, field service, installations, inspections or maintenance."},{"question":"How long does an automation like this take to implement?","answer":"Scope drives the timeline, but projects of this shape are measured in weeks rather than months, because they build on an existing platform instead of standing up a new one. The heavier part is usually the operational diagnosis — mapping how evidence really flows — not the code itself."}],"seoTitle":"Case study: automated proof-of-delivery with Google Apps Script","seoDescription":"A same-day flower and gift retailer lost delivery evidence in WhatsApp threads and personal phones. We automated capture, filing and lookup inside Google Workspace — no new app for couriers to learn."}},{"frontmatter":{"title":"How an international hardware distributor went from invisible on Google to thousands of organic visits a month","slug":"technical-seo-b2b-hardware-catalog-laravel","category":"retail-and-ecommerce","services":["seo","custom-software-development","web-development"],"techStack":[{"name":"Laravel","category":"Framework"},{"name":"PHP","category":"Language"},{"name":"Schema.org","category":"Structured Data"},{"name":"Open Graph","category":"Structured Data"},{"name":"XML Sitemaps","category":"Technical SEO"},{"name":"Google Search Console","category":"Measurement"}],"year":2025,"country":"United States","language":"en","translationKey":"technical-seo-hardware-catalog","featured":true,"status":"published","publishedDate":"2026-07-18","lastUpdated":"2026-07-18","client":{"name":"International B2B hardware distributor","sector":"B2B hardware distribution","geography":"United States · Peru · Chile · expanding to Colombia and Dubai","bio":"This client is an international hardware distributor with operations across the United States, Peru and Chile, and active expansion into Colombia and Dubai. Their commercial platform — built in Laravel by a third party — carries thousands of products identified by Part Number, SKU and UPC.","anonymous":true,"sameAs":[]},"hero":{"lead":"An international hardware distributor had thousands of products published and almost no presence on Google. Buyers searching by Part Number, SKU or UPC — the way B2B hardware is actually sourced — found nothing. Rather than rebuild a working Laravel platform, we engineered a custom SEO module into it. In weeks, more than 8,000 pages began indexing correctly and organic traffic went from negligible to thousands of monthly visits.","visual":"/case-studies/covers/technical-seo-hardware-catalog.webp","visualType":"image","visualAlt":"A search-results interface where the distributor's catalog listing surfaces and rises to the top — from invisible to found on Google","stats":[{"value":"8,000+","label":"Pages indexed","context":"from near-zero indexation"},{"value":"Thousands","label":"Monthly organic visits","context":"where there had been almost none"},{"value":"Weeks","label":"To first results","context":"not months"},{"value":"0","label":"Platform rebuilds","context":"existing Laravel system preserved"}]},"diagnosis":{"challenges":["Thousands of products published but practically absent from Google","Products were not being indexed correctly","Searches by Part Number, SKU or UPC generated no traffic — the exact way B2B buyers search","The platform lacked technical SEO fundamentals altogether","The system was built by a third party and had to keep running untouched","Competing organically in an established international hardware market"],"businessImpact":"A catalog invisible to search is a catalog that only sells to buyers who already know the company. For a distributor expanding into new markets, that ceiling caps growth precisely where new demand should come from.","previousAttempts":"The platform had been developed by a third party with no SEO foundation in its architecture. The objective was explicitly to increase organic visibility WITHOUT replacing the existing system."},"phases":[{"name":"Full technical audit","description":"We started by auditing the existing project rather than proposing a rebuild. We analyzed the Laravel architecture, how product routes were generated, what search engines could actually crawl and index, and where the structural gaps were. The audit made the diagnosis concrete: this was not a content problem, it was a technical foundations problem.","deliverables":["Complete technical SEO audit of the Laravel platform","Indexation and crawlability analysis","Prioritized gap list mapped to the existing architecture"]},{"name":"SEO module design for Laravel","description":"We designed a fully custom SEO module for Laravel, built to respect the existing architecture. The constraint drove the design: every improvement had to slot into the running system without disrupting day-to-day business operations or requiring the third-party platform to be re-engineered.","deliverables":["Custom SEO module architecture for Laravel","URL strategy covering product name, Part Number, SKU and UPC","Structured data and metadata model"]},{"name":"Implementation","description":"We implemented the module against the live platform: XML sitemaps, friendly URLs, canonical tags, dynamic metadata, Schema Markup, Open Graph, and indexation improvements — all wired into the existing product data so thousands of pages inherited them automatically rather than being edited one by one.","deliverables":["XML sitemaps and indexation improvements","Friendly URLs by product name, Part Number, SKU and UPC","Canonical tags, dynamic metadata, Schema Markup and Open Graph"]},{"name":"Measurement and next phases","description":"With Google Search Console as the source of truth, we tracked indexation as pages entered the index and organic traffic followed. Results arrived within weeks, and the client extended the engagement into new phases focused on content SEO and B2B positioning.","deliverables":["Search Console monitoring of indexation and organic performance","Roadmap for content SEO and B2B positioning phases"]}],"kpis":[{"label":"Pages indexed","value":"8,000+","context":"correctly indexed after implementation"},{"label":"Monthly organic visits","before":"Practically none","after":"Thousands","context":"reached within weeks"},{"label":"Time to first results","value":"Weeks","context":"not the usual multi-month SEO horizon"},{"label":"Platform rebuilds","value":"Zero","context":"the existing Laravel system stayed in production"}],"resultsEvidence":{"src":"/case-studies/evidence/technical-seo-search-console.webp","alt":"Google Search Console: daily organic clicks and impressions flat near zero for three months, then a sharp sustained rise","caption":"Google Search Console — daily clicks and impressions. Three flat months, then the step change once the module shipped."},"takeaways":[{"title":"Invisible on Google is usually an architecture problem, not a content problem","body":"The distributor already had thousands of products published. Nothing was missing from the catalog — what was missing was the technical foundation that lets a search engine crawl, understand and index it. Adding more content would not have moved a single position.","applicability":"Any ecommerce or catalog platform with substantial inventory and disproportionately low organic traffic."},{"title":"B2B buyers search by identifier, not by adjective","body":"In hardware distribution, a buyer searches a Part Number, SKU or UPC — not a marketing description. Building dedicated URLs for each identifier aligned the platform with how purchasing actually happens in this market.","applicability":"Distributors, industrial suppliers and any B2B catalog where products carry standardized identifiers."},{"title":"You can modernize the SEO layer without replacing the platform","body":"The system was built by a third party and running the business. We engineered the SEO capability into that architecture rather than around it — no rebuild, no migration, no operational disruption.","applicability":"Businesses running on functional legacy or third-party platforms they cannot afford to pause."},{"title":"Structural fixes compound across the whole catalog at once","body":"Because the improvements were wired into the product data model, thousands of pages inherited them simultaneously. That is why results appeared in weeks rather than the multi-month horizon typical of content-led SEO.","applicability":"Large catalogs where per-page manual optimization is economically impossible."}],"faqs":[{"question":"Can you improve SEO on a Laravel platform built by another vendor?","answer":"Yes. In this project the Laravel platform was developed by a third party and had to keep running untouched. We audited the existing architecture and built a custom SEO module that slotted into it — sitemaps, friendly URLs, canonical tags, dynamic metadata, Schema Markup and Open Graph — without replacing the system or interrupting daily operations."},{"question":"Why do thousands of published products still get no organic traffic?","answer":"Because publishing is not indexing. If the platform lacks technical SEO fundamentals — XML sitemaps, crawlable friendly URLs, canonical tags, dynamic metadata and structured data — search engines cannot properly discover, understand or index the catalog. The products exist for a visitor who already has the URL, and effectively do not exist for a search engine."},{"question":"How do you make a B2B catalog rank for Part Number, SKU and UPC searches?","answer":"By giving each identifier its own crawlable, canonical URL and describing the product with structured data. B2B buyers search hardware by exact identifier rather than by descriptive phrases, so the platform has to expose a dedicated, indexable page for each one — which is precisely what the SEO module implemented for this distributor."},{"question":"How long does technical SEO take to show results on an ecommerce catalog?","answer":"When the problem is structural rather than editorial, results can appear quickly. The distributor saw more than 8,000 pages begin indexing and organic traffic reach thousands of monthly visits within weeks, because the fixes were wired into the data model and applied to the entire catalog simultaneously rather than page by page."},{"question":"Is it better to rebuild the platform or add technical SEO to it?","answer":"It depends on whether the platform works. The distributor's Laravel system was functioning as a business tool — the gap was purely SEO capability. Rebuilding would have added cost, migration risk and downtime to solve a problem that engineering into the existing architecture solved without any of it. Rebuild when the platform is the constraint; engineer when the platform is fine and the capability is missing."}],"seoTitle":"Case study: technical SEO for a Laravel B2B hardware catalog","seoDescription":"An international B2B hardware distributor had thousands of products and almost no organic traffic. We built a custom SEO module for their existing Laravel platform — 8,000+ pages indexed and thousands of monthly visits, without rebuilding the system."}}],"total":2,"label":"cases","title":"More cases","countLabel":"2 cases"}]}]
